<section>
	<article>
		<h3>The Future of Open Broadcast as Web-Radio and -Platform</h3>=20
		<p>
			After careful consideration, we have decided to no longer broadcast
			the program of Open Broadcast on DAB digital radio. Since early 2010,
			Open Broadcast was digitally receivable in the agglomerations of=20
			Zurich, Basel and Bern. In the future, the program will continue to=20
			be available worldwide trough our=20
			<a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110727125825/http://www.openbro=
adcast.ch/mod/medialibrary/api/cast/listen">
			web stream</a>. The DAB signal is turned off in the next few days.
		</p><p>
			The reasons why we decided to abandon DAB broadcasting, are the high
			costs involved. Under the current license conditions, legal digital=20
			radio is only affordable to those who are ready to spend several=20
			hundred-thousand swiss-francs per year. Under these conditions our=20
			company is no longer willing to pay for a technology, which could be=20
			available for a more favorable price.
		</p><p>
			Starting mid-May, the Open Broadcast Platform will also be used by=20
			<a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110727125825/http://unikomradio=
s.ch/" title=3D"UNIKOM">non-commercial=20
			radio stations</a> as a=20
			<a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20110727125825/http://blog.openbr=
oadcast.ch/en/openbroadcast/unikom/" title=3D"Project Unikom">promotional p=
latform</a> for new music=20
			releases as well as a collective digital archive.
		</p>
	</article>
</section>

